The benchmark search results repeatedly returned the same four sources: the Department of Justice indictment, an NPR report describing the Senate findings, Senate Intelligence Committee Volume II, and the underlying Oxford/Graphika report released through the Senate Intelligence Committee. The benchmark excerpts for the platform-specific searches did not return separate official Facebook, Twitter, Reddit, Google, or YouTube source URLs.

---

## **Fully Comprehensive Findings**

### 1. Department of Justice indictment: organizational structure, funding, deception, infrastructure, objectives, and alleged conduct

The primary charging document is **United States v. Internet Research Agency LLC et al., Criminal No. 1:18-cr-00032-DLF, Indictment, filed February 16, 2018**, a 37-page document. The document identifies the following defendants:

- Internet Research Agency LLC, also known as Mediasintez LLC, Glavset LLC, Mixinfo LLC, Azimut LLC, and Novinfo LLC;
- Concord Management and Consulting LLC;
- Concord Catering;
- Yevgeniy Viktorovich Prigozhin;
- Mikhail Ivanovich Bystrov;
- Mikhail Leonidovich Burchik, also known as Mikhail Abramov;
- Aleksandra Yuryevna Krylova;
- Anna Vladislavovna Bogacheva;
- Sergey Pavlovich Polozov;
- Maria Anatolyevna Bovda, also known as Maria Anatolyevna Belyaeva;
- Robert Sergeyevich Bovda;
- Dzhey­khun Nasimi Ogly Aslanov, also known as Jayhoon Aslanov and Jay Aslanov;
- Vadim Vladimirovich Podkopaev;
- Gleb Igorevich Vasilchenko;
- Irina Viktorovna Kaverzina; and
- Vladimir Venkov. [1, pp. 1–2]

The indictment states that the Internet Research Agency was “a Russian organization engaged in operations to interfere with elections and political processes.” It alleges that the named individuals “worked in various capacities to carry out Defendant ORGANIZATION’s interference operations targeting the United States.” It further alleges that, “from in or around 2014 to the present,” the defendants knowingly and intentionally conspired with one another and with persons known and unknown to defraud the United States by impairing, obstructing, and defeating lawful government functions “through fraud and deceit for the purpose of interfering with the U.S. political and electoral processes, including the presidential election of 2016.” [1, ¶ 2, pp. 2–3]

The indictment alleges that the Internet Research Agency began operations to interfere with the U.S. political system, including the 2016 presidential election, “as early as 2014.” It states that the organization received funding from Yevgeniy Viktorovich Prigozhin and companies he controlled, including Concord Management and Consulting LLC and Concord Catering. The indictment collectively calls those companies and Prigozhin “CONCORD.” It alleges that Concord and Prigozhin “spent significant funds” to further the organization’s operations and to pay the remaining defendants, along with other uncharged organization employees, salaries and bonuses for their work at the organization. [1, ¶ 3, p. 3]

The indictment alleges that defendants posed as U.S. persons and created false U.S. personas. They operated social-media pages and groups designed to attract U.S. audiences. The pages and groups addressed divisive U.S. political and social issues and falsely claimed to be controlled by U.S. activists, when in fact they were controlled by the defendants. The defendants also allegedly used the stolen identities of real U.S. persons to post on organization-controlled social-media accounts. Over time, the indictment alleges, these accounts became a means to reach significant numbers of Americans for the purpose of interfering with the U.S. political system, including the 2016 presidential election. [1, ¶ 4, p. 3]

The indictment further alleges that certain defendants traveled to the United States under false pretenses to collect intelligence intended to inform the organization’s operations. The defendants allegedly procured and used computer infrastructure, based partly in the United States, to hide the Russian origin of their activities and avoid detection by U.S. regulators and law enforcement. [1, ¶ 5, p. 3]

The indictment describes the organization’s strategic goal as sowing discord in the U.S. political system, including the 2016 presidential election. It alleges that the defendants posted derogatory information about a number of candidates and that, by early to mid-2016, their operations included supporting the presidential campaign of then-candidate Donald J. Trump and disparaging Hillary Clinton. [1, ¶ 6, p. 4]

The defendants allegedly made expenditures to carry out those activities, including purchasing political advertisements on social media in the names of U.S. persons and entities. They allegedly staged political rallies inside the United States while posing as U.S. grassroots entities and U.S. persons and while concealing their Russian identities and affiliation with the Internet Research Agency. The indictment alleges that they solicited and compensated real U.S. persons to promote or disparage candidates. It also alleges that some defendants, posing as U.S. persons and concealing their Russian association, communicated with unwitting individuals associated with the Trump campaign and with other political activists to seek to coordinate political activities. [1, ¶ 6, p. 4]

The indictment alleges that the defendants sought to avoid detection of their Russian affiliation by obstructing lawful functions of the U.S. government through fraud and deceit. The alleged conduct included making expenditures in connection with the 2016 presidential election without proper regulatory disclosure; failing to register as foreign agents conducting political activities in the United States; and obtaining visas through false and fraudulent statements. [1, ¶ 7, p. 4]

The indictment charges conspiracy to defraud the United States under 18 U.S.C. §§ 2, 371, 1349, and 1028A. [1, cover and ¶ 8]

These statements are allegations contained in an indictment. The excerpt does not establish that every allegation resulted in a conviction, adjudicated finding, or judicial determination. [1]

### 2. Senate Intelligence Committee Volume II: official assessment of the campaign

The Senate Select Committee on Intelligence report is titled **Report of the Select Committee on Intelligence, United States Senate, on Russian Active Measures Campaigns and Interference in the 2016 U.S. Election, Volume II: Russia’s Use of Social Media, with Additional Views**. The benchmark copy is identified as a 116th Congress, 1st Session, Senate Report 116-XX document. [3, cover]

The report’s table of contents identifies the following sections and page locations:

- I. Introduction, p. 3;
- II. Findings, p. 4;
- III. The Reach of Social Media, p. 8;
- IV. Russian Use of Disinformation, p. 11;
  - A. Russian Active Measures, p. 12;
  - B. Russia’s Military and Information Warfare, p. 13;
  - C. Russia’s Weaponization of Social Media, p. 14;
  - D. Russian Social Media Tactics, p. 15;
  - E. Features of Russian Active Measures, p. 20;
- V. The Internet Research Agency, p. 22;
  - A. Yevgeniy Prigozhin and the Kremlin, p. 23;
  - B. IRA Operations, p. 24;
  - C. The Role of the IRA Troll, p. 25;
  - D. Troll Narratives, p. 28;
- VI. IRA Activities Against the United States in 2016, p. 29;
  - A. Origins of IRA Activity in the United States, p. 29;
  - B. IRA Operations Explicitly Targeting the 2016 U.S. Election, p. 32;
  - C. Other IRA Operations Targeting U.S. Politicians and Society, p. 37;
  - D. IRA Use of Paid Advertisements, p. 40;
  - E. The IRA Information Warfare Campaign, p. 41;
  - F. Ongoing IRA Activities, p. 42;
- VII. IRA Use of Social Media by Platform, p. 43;
- VIII. Other Russian Social Media Information Warfare Efforts, p. 63;
  - A. Main Intelligence Directorate (GRU), p. 63;
  - B. [heading truncated in benchmark excerpt], p. 69;
  - C. Other Russian Government Activities, p. 70;
- IX. U.S. Government Response, p. 71;
- X. The Committee’s Review of Russia’s Use of Social Media, p. 76;
- XI. Recommendations, p. 78;
  - A. Industry Measures, p. 78;
  - B. Congressional Measures, p. 80;
  - C. Executive Branch Measures, p. 81;
  - D. Other Measures, p. 81; and
- XII. Additional Views of Senator Wyden, p. 83. [3, table of contents, pp. 1–2]

The report’s introduction states:

> “In 2016, Russian operatives associated with the St. Petersburg-based Internet Research Agency (IRA) used social media to conduct an information warfare campaign designed to spread disinformation and societal division in the United States.” [3, introduction, p. 3]

The report states that, while masquerading as Americans, the operatives used targeted advertisements, intentionally falsified news articles, self-generated content, and social-media platform tools to interact with and attempt to deceive tens of millions of social-media users in the United States. It states that the campaign sought to polarize Americans based on societal, ideological, and racial differences, provoked real-world events, and was part of a foreign government’s covert support for Russia’s favored candidate in the U.S. presidential election. [3, introduction, p. 3]

The report defines “information warfare,” for purposes of the volume, as Russia’s strategy for the use and management of information to pursue a competitive advantage. It cites the Congressional Research Service’s **Defense Primer: Information Operations**, dated December 18, 2018. [3, footnote 1, p. 3]

The Committee states that it undertook its study pursuant to its congressional mandate to oversee and conduct oversight of U.S. government intelligence activities and programs, including the effectiveness of the Intelligence Community’s counterintelligence function. The Committee states that its findings drew on the work of professional Committee staff and input from cybersecurity professionals, social-media companies, U.S. law-enforcement and intelligence agencies, and researchers and experts in social-network analysis, political content, disinformation, hate speech, algorithms, and automation working under the auspices of the Committee’s Technical Advisory Group. [3, introduction, p. 3]

The report states that the Technical Advisory Group was an external group of experts consulted by the Committee for substantive technical advice. For this matter, the Committee requested assistance from two independent working groups with technical capabilities and expertise needed to analyze the data. The working groups were led by three Technical Advisory Group members: John Kelly, founder and CEO of the social-media analytics firm Graphika; Phil Howard, an academic researcher at the Oxford Internet Institute; and Renee DiResta, Director of Research at New Knowledge, a cybersecurity company focused on protecting the public sphere from disinformation attacks. [3, footnote 3, p. 3]

The Committee states that the Technical Advisory Group researchers released two public reports on the IRA’s information-warfare campaign based on data provided to the Committee by social-media companies. The report identifies one of those reports as **The Tactics and Tropes of the Internet Research Agency**, by Renee DiResta, Kris Shaffer, Becky Ruppel, David Sullivan, Robert Matney, Ryan Fox, Jonathan Albright, and Ben Johnson, dated December 17, 2018. It identifies the other as **The IRA, Social Media and Political Polarization in the United States, 2012–2018**, by Phil Howard, Bharath Ganesh, Dimitra Liotsiou, John Kelly, and Camille François, dated December 2018. [3, footnote 4, p. 3]

### 3. Reach, platforms, timing, and operational characteristics

The Oxford/Graphika report released by the Senate Intelligence Committee is titled **The IRA, Social Media and Political Polarization in the United States, 2012–2018**. Its listed authors are Philip N. Howard, Bharath Ganesh, Dimitra Liotsiou, John Kelly, and Camille François. The repository identifies the document as an article, with a version date of October 2019, and states that it was released by the Select Committee on Intelligence, United States Senate, in October 2019. [4]

The report’s abstract states that Russia’s Internet Research Agency “launched an extended attack on the United States by using computational propaganda to misinform and polarize US voters.” It describes itself as the first major analysis of the attack based on data provided by social-media firms to the Senate Select Committee on Intelligence. [4]

The report states that it investigates how the IRA exploited Facebook, Instagram, Twitter, and YouTube, among other platforms, to impact U.S. users. It identifies which aspects of the IRA’s campaign strategy received the most traction on social media and the means by which the IRA microtargeted U.S. voters with particular messages. [4]

The report states:

- “Between 2013 and 2018, the IRA’s Facebook, Instagram, and Twitter campaigns reached tens of millions of users in the United States.” [4]
- “Over 30 million users, between 2015 and 2017, shared the IRA’s Facebook and Instagram posts with their friends and family, liking, reacting to, and commenting on them along the way.” [4]
- Peaks in advertising and organic activity often corresponded to important dates in the U.S. political calendar, crises, and international events. [4]
- IRA activities focused on the United States began on Twitter in 2013 but quickly evolved into a multi-platform strategy involving Facebook, Instagram, and YouTube, among other platforms. [4]
- “The most far reaching IRA activity is in organic posting, not advertisements.” [4]

The report states that the IRA’s activities were designed to polarize the U.S. public and interfere in elections by:

- campaigning for African American voters to boycott elections or follow the wrong voting procedures in 2016;
- more recently campaigning for Mexican American and Hispanic voters to distrust U.S. institutions;
- encouraging extreme right-wing voters to be more confrontational; and
- spreading sensationalist, conspiratorial, and other forms of junk political news and misinformation to voters across the political spectrum. [4]

The report states that the campaigns did not stop once Russia’s IRA was caught interfering in the 2016 election. Instead, engagement rates increased and covered a widening range of public-policy issues, national-security issues, and issues pertinent to younger voters. [4]

The report identifies April 2017 as the highest peak of IRA advertising volume on Facebook. It associates that month with the Syrian missile strike, the use of the “Mother of All Bombs” on ISIS tunnels in eastern Afghanistan, and the release of the tax-reform plan. [4]

The report states that IRA posts on Instagram and Facebook increased substantially after the election, with Instagram experiencing the greatest increase in IRA activity. [4]

The report states that IRA accounts actively engaged with disinformation and practices common to Russian “trolling.” Some posts referred to Russian troll factories that flooded online conversations with posts; some denied being Russian trolls; and some complained about the platforms’ alleged political biases when facing account suspension. [4]

### 4. Race-focused targeting and activity after Election Day

An NPR report dated October 8, 2019, titled **Senate Report Finds Russians Used Social Media To Target Race In 2016**, describes the Senate Intelligence Committee’s findings. The article states that the Russian government’s efforts to interfere in the 2016 elections focused on African American audiences. [2]

The NPR report states that, using Facebook pages, Instagram content, and Twitter posts, Russian information operatives working for the Internet Research Agency had an “overwhelming operational emphasis on race” and that “no single group of Americans was targeted … more than African Americans.” [2]

The article states that over 66 percent of the IRA’s Facebook content contained a term related to race. [2]

It states that location-targeted advertisements were “principally aimed at African-Americans in key metropolitan areas.” [2]

The article identifies pages such as **Blacktivist** as a focus of the Russian information operation and states that Blacktivist garnered **11.2 million engagements** with Facebook users. [2]

The NPR report states that the Senate report found increased activity after Election Day in 2016. It reports the following post-election increases:

- Instagram activity increased 238 percent;
- Facebook activity increased 59 percent;
- Twitter activity increased 52 percent; and
- YouTube citations increased 84 percent. [2]

The article states that the Senate Committee’s work affirmed conclusions from earlier investigations, including the Mueller report and the January 6, 2017, Intelligence Community Assessment. [2]

It quotes the Committee as stating:

> “The Committee found that IRA social media activity was overtly and almost invariably supportive of then-candidate Trump.” [2]

It also quotes the Committee as stating:

> “The Committee found that the Russian government tasked and supported the IRA’s interference in the 2016 U.S. election.” [2]

The NPR report states that the Senate Committee found that the Internet Research Agency sought to harm Hillary Clinton and support Donald Trump “at the direction of the Kremlin.” [2]

It also states that the Committee concluded that the IRA sought to focus on socially divisive issues, including race, to pit Americans against one another. [2]

The article quotes Senate Intelligence Committee Chairman Richard Burr as saying:

> “By flooding social media with false reports, conspiracy theories, and trolls, and by exploiting existing divisions, Russia is trying to breed distrust of our democratic institutions
